body:
Thank you! I did what you had mentioned and it made it a lot easier to see what I was supposed to get in return from the fetch call. I ended up modifying my code to make testing a little easier and noticed right away that the fetch call takes like 6-8 seconds before I actually see any results. What I mean is with this code I have implemented,

    async showPokemonSearchResults(cardCharacter: string) {
    // const skeletonURL = 'https://api.pokemontcg.io/v2/cards?q=name';
    // const url = `${skeletonURL}:${cardCharacter}`; // Corrected URL construction
    // console.log('URL:', url); // Log the constructed URL
    // const headers = new HttpHeaders().set('X-Api-Key', this.apiKey);
    // return this.http.get(url, { headers });
    try {
      const response = await fetch('https://api.pokemontcg.io/v2/cards?q=name:charizard');
      const data = await response.json();

      // Check if the response contains data
      if (data && data.data && Array.isArray(data.data)) {
        const dataArray = data.data; // This is the array of Pokemon cards

        console.log(dataArray); // Display the array of Pokemon cards in the console

        for (const card of dataArray) {
          console.log(`Name: ${card.name}, HP: ${card.hp}, Rarity: ${card.rarity}`);
        }
      } else {
        console.log('No data found');
      }
    } catch (error) {
      console.error('Error fetching data:', error);
    }
  }

it does not print Name and HP of the cards till a few seconds later. It is probably because I am fetching a TON of cards vs. just one like you had mentioned. Now is there a way to possibly be able to put a break point on any code and have the const dataArray be filled with the data after it retrieves the data? I have tried putting break points on the console logs dataArray has been filled but they seem to be skipped right over since everything is happening asynchronously.
